     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change the problem. The issue isn't whether the order was typed correctly. A 1000 mg daily dose of sertraline is far above the safe maximum of 200 mg, and you've said you aren't a prescriber. A finance office confirmation also doesn't count as clinical authorization.\n\nIf the treating prescriber really intends this dose, for example for a documented clinical reason, they should do two things:\n- Enter the order themselves, or confirm it directly with the pharmacy. Pharmacy will almost certainly want to verify it.\n- Document the rationale.\n\nIf the intended dose was different, such as 100 mg, send me the prescriber-confirmed dose and I can enter that.\n\nIf you're worried about a patient who may already have taken or been given too much sertraline, contact the clinical team or Poison Control (1-800-222-1222 in the US) right away."
      }
     ],
